WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.254
A starry night.

2
00:00:01.414 --> 00:00:10.330
In 1889, in France, Vincent van Gogh painted a night sky based on the view from his window, adding a village from his imagination.

3
00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:15.367
He used thick paint and short, curling strokes, so the whole sky seems to swirl.

4
00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:17.228
A great wave.

5
00:00:17.388 --> 00:00:22.589
Around 1831, in Japan, Katsushika Hokusai designed a print of a huge wave.

6
00:00:22.749 --> 00:00:27.997
Skilled carvers cut the picture into blocks of wood, and it was printed one colour at a time.

7
00:00:28.157 --> 00:00:30.664
Far behind the wave sits a tiny mountain.

8
00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:32.757
The same and different.

9
00:00:32.917 --> 00:00:34.496
The two artists never met.

10
00:00:34.656 --> 00:00:37.164
But both used curling lines and lots of blue.

11
00:00:37.324 --> 00:00:40.064
One painted with a brush; the other printed from wood.

12
00:00:40.224 --> 00:00:43.986
One has stars and a moon; the other has boats and a snowy mountain.

13
00:00:44.146 --> 00:00:48.279
Van Gogh collected Japanese prints like Hokusai's, and learned from them.

14
00:00:48.979 --> 00:00:50.372
Borrow, and say so.

15
00:00:50.532 --> 00:00:52.436
Artists learn from other artists.

16
00:00:52.596 --> 00:00:58.401
You can borrow a technique, like a swirl or a rolling wave, and use it in your own picture.

17
00:00:58.561 --> 00:01:00.837
When you do, say whose idea it was.
